Model contract:
- Input: images, FLOAT/float32, static [1, 3, 640, 640], RGB in NCHW order,
  channel values divided by 255. Preserve aspect ratio when resizing and
  map detections back through the application's letterbox and tile geometry.
- Output: output0, FLOAT/float32, [1, 5, 8400]. Channels are center_x,
  center_y, width, height in input pixels, and sigmoid face probability.
  There is one class; no separate objectness multiplier is required.
- Raw one-to-many predictions, end2end=False. No embedded score threshold,
  TopK or NMS; the application performs confidence filtering and NMS.
- The checkpoint uses a Pose head with five 2D face landmarks. This export
  retains only its first five detection channels. The unused landmark branch
  is removed from the graph; no landmark computation or output is retained.
- Weights and eligible intermediate tensors use FP16. Input and output stay
  FP32; Resize operators remain FP32. ONNX opset 18.

Reproduction:
1. Download the source checkpoint from the release URL above and verify its
   SHA256 before loading it. No training dataset is needed for this export.
2. Use Python 3.14.8, torch 2.14.1, ultralytics 8.4.177, onnx 1.23.2,
   onnxslim 0.1.98 and onnxconverter-common 1.16.0. The validation runtime
   used here was onnxruntime 1.31.0 with CPUExecutionProvider.
3. Load the checkpoint with ultralytics.YOLO, set model.model.end2end=False,
   and call model.export(format="onnx", imgsz=640, batch=1, dynamic=False,
   simplify=True, opset=18, device="cpu", nms=None, quantize=None).
   In this Ultralytics version nms=None selects raw one-to-many output.
4. The exported Pose output is [1,15,8400]. Use ONNX shape inference to verify
   that its final axis-1 Concat combines 4 box, 1 score and 10 landmark
   channels. Remove the 10-channel input from that Concat, change the output
   shape to [1,5,8400], clear graph.value_info, and run onnxslim.slim.
   Check that the unused landmark branch has been removed. The retained
   values are exactly the original Pose output's first five channels.
5. Clear graph.value_info again. Convert with
   onnxconverter_common.float16.convert_float_to_float16 using
   keep_io_types=True and DEFAULT_OP_BLOCK_LIST plus "Resize".
   Save as yolo26n-face-640-fp16.onnx.
6. Run onnx.checker.check_model and verify finite native-runtime output and
   agreement against the first five PyTorch output channels. The packaged
   file passed these checks on black, gray and seeded synthetic inputs.
   Export metadata includes a timestamp, so regenerated file hashes may
   differ even when the model computation is identical.

